The Scale-Down Cooldown (Stabilization Window)

When the load generator stops, CPU utilization drops back down to 0% within seconds, 
but the extra Pods will remain running for 5 minutes by default.

This delay is governed by the Stabilization Window (stabilizationWindowSeconds: 300):

The Problem (Thrashing / Flapping): In production, network traffic is often bursty. 
If Kubernetes scaled pods down immediately after a 10-second traffic dip, an incoming second wave of
 traffic would overwhelm the single remaining pod before new pods could boot up again.

The Solution: The HPA controller looks at the highest load calculated over the past 5-minute rolling
 window before removing any Pods. Once the metrics remain consistently below target for the entire 
 duration, it begins scaling down.

Under-the-Hood Sequence During Scale-Down

[ Load Stops ]
      |
      v
1. Metrics Server reports 0% CPU utilization
      |
      v
2. HPA Controller recalculates target replicas:
   ceil[ currentReplicas * (0 / 50) ] = 0 -> clamped to --min=1
      |
      v
3. Stabilization Timer starts (300 seconds / 5 min cooldown)
      |
      v
4. Cooldown expires -> HPA updates Deployment spec: replicas = 1
      |
      v
5. ReplicaSet terminates excess Pods (sends SIGTERM -> graceful shutdown)





